{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "All's Well That Ends Well"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "New Starts"}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 16}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange information about unlikely events or fairly certain conditions that will be fulfilled in present, past or future  in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn11le16ob01re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Perfect Modals"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Could have</span>, <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">should have</span>, <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">would have</span></span> to talk about hypothetical things. It means, things that did not really happen in the past. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "The structure of a sentence that uses Perfect modals is:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Subject + modal + have (not) + past participle"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<br/>COULD HAVE+PAST PARTICIPLE <br/><br/>We use this structure to: <br/><br/><ul> <li>Talk about something that was possible in the past or you had the ability to do it, but you did not do it. <br/><br/>For example: </br> </br>  <span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">could have gone to</span> France with my friends, but I didn't save any money.</span><br/><br/></li> <li>Make a guess about some event that happened in the past. <br/><br/>Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">You know her, she <span class=\"strong\">could have been caught up</span> in an in-depth conversation with one of her clients.</span></li> </ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "So she couldn't have been held up at work."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Amy might have been delayed at work.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "She could have been caught up in an in-depth conversation with one of her clients."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You must not have understood what she meant."}, {"type": "text", "content": "COULDN'T HAVE+PAST PARTICIPLE <br/><br/> We use this structure to <br/><br/><ul> <li>talk about something that was not possible in the past, even if you had wanted to do it. <br/><br/> Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">couldn't have bought</span> that house. It was too expensive!</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "SHOULD HAVE+PAST PARTICIPLE <br/><br/> We use this structure to:  <br/><br/><ul> <li>talk about some idea that would have been a good one, but you did not do it. <br/><br/>Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">should have studied</span> harder to pass the test.</span><br/><br/></li><li>talk about something that has already happened under normal conditions.<br/><br/>  Example:<br/><br/><span class=\"italic\"> Camilo <span class=\"strong\">should have arrived</span> by now. He is so punctual.</span> (It means that he has not arrived). </li></ul>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "MAY/MIGHT HAVE+PAST PARTICIPLE<br/><br/>  We use this structure to<br/><br/> <ul> <li>talk about possibilities that have happened now or happened at some point in the past. <br/><br/> Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">may/might have arrived</span> hours ago. </span>(Perhaps she arrived hour ago.) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "We should have invited her to our party. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Concession/Contrast Clause"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use concession or contrast clauses to express an idea that suggests the opposite of the main part of the sentence. They are useful in both writing and speaking since they make your language more dynamic and diverse."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You introduce concession or contrast clauses with conjunctions like <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">although</span></span>, <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">though</span></span>, <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">even though</span></span>, <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">whereas</span></span>, <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">while</span></span> or <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">even if</span></span>. <br/><br/> Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Although</span>, we have been together a month now, we still haven't had a serious conversation about our relationship where things are going.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Although</span> and <span class=\"strong\">though</span> have similar meanings. <span class=\"strong\">Even though</span></span> is stronger in tone.<br/><br/>  Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Even though</span> there were a lot of cars on the road, traffic moved pretty smoothly today.</span><br/><br/>  You can use words like <span class=\"italic\">still</span>, <span class=\"italic\">nevertheless</span> or <span class=\"italic\">just the same</span> in the main clause to emphasize the contrast.<br/><br/>   <span class=\"strong\">Whereas</span> and <span class=\"strong\">while</span> are similar but <span class=\"strong\">whereas</span> is more formal.<br/><br/> Examples: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">She's just a little bit late <span class=\"strong\">whereas</span> you are the one who is usually lagging behind.</span><br/><br/> <span class=\"italic\">However, <span class=\"strong\">while</span> things seem fine, I sometimes get the feelings that Amy is a little ... restless. </span><br/><br/>  You use <span class=\"strong\">even if</span> to emphasize that the situation will not change.<br/><br/> Example: <br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">I probably wouldn't tell you <span class=\"strong\">even if</span> I knew anyway. </span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "However, while everything seems to be going well, I'm not sure of her true feelings. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Although, we have been dating for a while, I don't know where things stand."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Even though there were a lot of people lined up, we got into the venue pretty quickly."}, {"type": "example", "content": "He's only 5 minutes late whereas she is almost an hour late!"}, {"type": "example", "content": "She wouldn't come even if we told her he was going to be there!"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "in-depth"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "detailed, comprehensive and thorough"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: She could have been caught up in an in-depth conversation with one of her clients."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob01aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when we are waiting for a friend!"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to hold up"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be forced to wait"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: She couldn't have been held up at work."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob01aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to lag behind"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to fail to keep the expected pace"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You are the one who is usually lagging behind."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob01aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "uncertain"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not completely confident or sure of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: She said she had some news to tell me, so I'm a little uncertain."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob01aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "restless"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "unable to rest or relax as a result of anxiety or boredom"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I sometimes get the feeling that Amy is a little ... restless. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob01aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "paranoid"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "anxious because you do not \u200bfeel you can \u200btrust \u200bothers"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: David is paranoid about people listening to his phone calls."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob01aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I brainstorm ideas with a partner."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I discuss what are the most important things that people wait for."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write a 200 word story on the most important thing I've waited for."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I present my story to a partner."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I give feedback."}], 